  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nDry air will continue to spread northeastward through Arizona on\nFriday with low level mixing ratios dropping to around 8 g/kg over\nthe lower deserts to 9-10 g/kg over the eastern Arizona high\nterrain. These moisture levels during the summer monsoon season\ntypically prohibit convection across the area with at most a\n10-20% chance of isolated showers or weak storms across the far \neastern Arizona high terrain. However, even with the drying \nconditions surface dew points well into the 50s will still keep \nthat slight muggy feeling in the air. The trough will continue to \ninfluence the region through Friday keeping temperatures a few \ndegrees below normal under partly cloudy skies.\n\nSaturday looks to be quite similar to Friday with periods of \nhigher clouds persisting, but temperatures will begin to tick \nupward again as the subtropical high begins to nudge back into our\nregion. Guidance shows H5 heights gradually rising into a\n592-595dm range by early next week, or back to around the 90th \npercentile of climatology. Moisture levels look to stay fairly\nsteady for the rest of the weekend into early next week keeping at\nmost low grade monsoon storm chances in place. The increased\nheights and somewhat dry conditions will help to push daytime\nhighs to above normal by Monday with readings right around 110\ndegrees for next Monday and Tuesday.",
